"Latest Papers - October 24, 2025 · Issue #106 · bguanmath/ArXiv_MathNT · GitHub (https://github.com/bguanmath/ArXiv_MathNT/issues/106)\nciteturn6search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; github-actions ... With a healthy skepticism of this apparent oversight, and out of an abundance of caution, we used ChatGPT to vibe code a Lean proof of both Hall's and our counterexamples. ... We also discuss applications to the Zilber-Pink conjecture in $\\mathcal{A}_2$ that naturally follow from our discussion. ... We connect the existence of a ternary classical universal quadratic form over a totally real number field $K$ with the property that all totally positive multiples of 2 are sums of squares (if $K$ does not contain $\\sqrt 2$ or contains a nonsquare totally positive unit). ... ### [2306.14389]  Higher Dimer Covers on Snake Graphs\nWe establish that {1, 2, 4, 8, 13} is a counterexample to this conjecture. During the preparation of this paper, we discovered that although this problem was presumed to be open for half a century, Marshall Hall, Jr. published a different counterexample three decades before Erd\\H{o}s first posed the problem. With a healthy skepticism of this apparent oversight, and out of an abundance of caution, we used ChatGPT to vibe code a Lean proof of both Hall's and our counterexamples.\n\n### [2012.10766]  Selberg's Central Limit Theorem for automorphic $L$-functions in the $t$-aspect\n\n  |  Date: 2025-10-22   |   Authors: Madhuparna Das   |   Category: math.NT   |  \n\n> Comment: The second mollified moments are not properly computed. Also, the inverse convolution of \\lambda_f(n) is incorrectly defined. Please withdraw all versions\n\nAbstract\n\nWe present a proof of Selberg's Central Limit Theorem for automorphic $L$-functions of degree 2 using Radziwi\\l\\l\\space and Soundararajan's method. Additionally, we prove the independence of the automorphic $L$-functions associated with the sequence of primitive holomorphic cusp forms.\n\n### [2510.19727]  Resolution of two conjectures by Erdős and Hall concerning separable numbers\n\n  |  Date: 2025-10-22   |   Authors: Stijn Cambie, Wouter van Doorn   |   Category: math.NT   |  \n\n> Comment: 5 Pages\n\nAbstract\n\nErd\\H{o}s and Hall defined a pair $(m, n)$ of positive integers to be interlocking, if between any pair of consecutive divisors (both larger than $1$) of $n$ (resp. $m$) there is a divisor of $m$ (resp. $n$). A positive integer is said to be separable if it belongs to an interlocking pair. We prove that the lower density of separable powers of two is positive, as well as the lower density of powers of two which are not separable. Finally, we prove that the number of interlocking pairs whose product is equal to the product of the first primes, is finite. We hereby resolve two conjectures by Erd\\H{o}s and Hall.\n\n### [2510.19703]  Identifying the simple finite-dimensional Lie algebras over C by means of simple sequences\n\n  |  Date: 2025-10-22   |   Authors: Kai Neergård   |   Category: math-ph   |  \n\n  |  Date: 2025-10-22   |   Authors: Vitezslav Kala, Kristyna Kramer, Jakub Krasensky   |   Category: math.NT   |  \n\n> Comment: 15 pages\n\nAbstract\n\nWe connect the existence of a ternary classical universal quadratic form over a totally real number field $K$ with the property that all totally positive multiples of 2 are sums of squares (if $K$ does not contain $\\sqrt 2$ or contains a nonsquare totally positive unit). In particular, we get that Kitaoka's Conjecture holds for all fields of odd discriminant.\n\n### [2306.14389]  Higher Dimer Covers on Snake Graphs\n\n  |  Date: 2025-10-22   |   Authors: Gregg Musiker, Nicholas Ovenhouse, Ralf Schiffler, Sylvester W. Zhang   |   Category: math.CO   |  \n\n> Comment: 31 pages, comments are welcome\n\nAbstract\n\nSnake graphs are a class of planar graphs that are important in the theory of cluster algebras. Indeed, the Laurent expansions of the cluster variables in cluster algebras from surfaces are given as weight generating functions for 1-dimer covers (or perfect matchings) of snake graphs. This gives rise to a canonical representation of the fundamental group, which recovers the braid group, acting on $D^b(\\mathfrak{M}_{\\eta})$.\n\n### [2510.11464]  On modular invariants of the truncated polynomial ring in rank four\n\n  |  Date: 2025-10-22   |   Authors: Dang Vo Phuc   |   Category: math.AC   |  \n\n> Comment: 25 pages. We welcome any constructive discussions and comments\n\nAbstract\n\nWe prove the rank-4 case of the conjecture of Ha-Hai-Nghia for the invariant subspace of the truncated polynomial ring $\\mathcal{Q}_m(n)=\\mathbb{F}_q[x_1,\\dots,x_n]/(x_1^{q^m},\\dots,x_n^{q^m}),$ under a new, explicit technical hypothesis. Our argument extends the determinant calculus for the delta operator by deriving crucial rank-4 identities governing its interaction with the Dickson algebra. We show that the proof of the conjecture reduces to a specific vanishing property, for which we introduce a sufficient condition, the \"matching hypothesis\" ($\\mathrm{H_{match}}$), relating the degree structures of Dickson invariants. This condition is justified by theoretical arguments and verified computationally in many cases. Combining this approach with the normalized derivation approach from our prior work, we establish the conjecture. As a result, the Lewis-Reiner-Stanton Conjecture is also confirmed for rank four under the given hypothesis.\n\n### [2510.19468]  Weight aspect asymptotic formula for Rankin-Selberg $L$-functions\n\n  |  Date: 2025-10-22   |   Authors: Aritra Ghosh   |   Category: math.NT   |  \n\nAbstract\n\nIn this article we show simultaneous non-vanishing of two Rankin-Selberg $L$-functions by proving an asymptotic result in weight aspect. The main input of this paper is to remove the $t$-integral from the result of Blomer-Harcos.\n\n### [2510.19449]  Fractals Emerging from the Toepltiz Determinants of the p-Cantor Sequence\n\n  |  Date: 2025-10-22   |   Authors: Steven Robertson, Noy Soffer Aranov   |   Category: math.NT   |  \n\n> Comment: 92 pages, 45 figures\n\nAbstract\n\nThis is the first of a pair of papers, whose collective goal is to disprove a conjecture of Kemarsky, Paulin, and Shapira (KPS) on the escape of mass of Laurent series. This paper lays the foundations on which its sibling builds. In particular, the $p$-Cantor sequence is introduced. This generalises the classical Cantor sequence into a $p$-automatic sequence for any odd prime $p$. Two main results are then established, both of which play a key role in the disproof of the KPS conjecture. First, the two-dimensional sequence comprised of the Toeplitz determinants of the $p$-Cantor sequence over $\\mathbb{F}_p$ is extensively studied. Indeed, the so-called profile of this sequence (which encodes the zero regions) is shown to be [p,p]-automatic. In the process of deriving this, the theory of so-called number walls is developed greatly. Many of these results are stated in full generality, as the authors expect them to be useful when tackling similar problems going forward. Secondly, a natural process is described that converts number wall of an automatic sequence into a unique fractal. When this sequence is the aforementioned $p$-Cantor sequence, this fractal is shown to have Hausdorff dimension $\\log((p^2+1)/2)/\\log(p).$\n\n### [2510.19417]  Escape of Mass of the $p$-Cantor Sequence\n\n  |  Date: 2025-10-22   |   Authors: Noy Soffer Aranov, Steven Robertson   |   Category: math.NT   |  \n\nAbstract\n\nLet $p$ be a prime. In 2017, Kemarsky, Paulin, and Shapira (KPS) conjectured that any Laurent series over $\\mathbb{F}_p$ exhibits full escape of mass with respect to any irreducible polynomial $P(t)\\in\\mathbb{F}_p[t]$. In 2025, this was shown to be false in the case $p=2$ and $P(t)=t$ by Nesharim, Shapira and the first named author. This work shows that for any odd prime $p$ and any irreducible polynomial $P(t)\\in\\mathbb{F}_p[t]$, the so-called $p$-Cantor sequence provides a counterexample to the aforementioned conjecture over $\\mathbb{F}_p$. Furthermore, the concepts of maximal escape of mass and generic escape of mass are introduced. These lead to two natural variations of the KPS conjecture, both of which are shown to hold for all previous counterexamples.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nau:Tan_T in:math - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?q=au%3ATan_T+in%3Amath)\nciteturn6search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Sep 06 2023 math.CO arXiv:2309.00757v1 ... @misc{2309.00757, author = {Imre Leader and Žarko Ran\\djelović and Ta Sheng Tan}, title = {{A} {N}ote on {H}amiltonian-{I}ntersecting {F}amilies of {G}raphs}, year = {2023}, eprint = {2309.00757}, note = {arXiv:2309.00757v1} }[Button: Copy Citation] ... We also show that a family of graphs such that any two have Hamiltonian intersection has size at most $1/2^n$ of all graphs, verifying a conjecture of the above authors.\n\nImre Leader, Žarko Ran\\djelović, Ta Sheng Tan\n\nSep 06 2023 math.CO arXiv:2309.00757v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2309.00757, author = {Imre Leader and Žarko Ran\\djelović and Ta Sheng Tan}, title = {{A} {N}ote on {H}amiltonian-{I}ntersecting {F}amilies of {G}raphs}, year = {2023}, eprint = {2309.00757}, note = {arXiv:2309.00757v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nHow many graphs on an $n$-point set can we find such that any two have connected intersection? Berger, Berkowitz, Devlin, Doppelt, Durham, Murthy and Vemuri showed that the maximum is exactly $1/2^{n-1}$ of all graphs. Our aim in this short note is to give a 'directed' version of this result; we show that a family of oriented graphs such that any two have strongly-connected intersection has size at most $1/3^n$ of all oriented graphs. We also show that a family of graphs such that any two have Hamiltonian intersection has size at most $1/2^n$ of all graphs, verifying a conjecture of the above authors.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ncombinatorics@uiuc - Suggested Reading (https://sites.google.com/site/uiuccombinatorics/home/suggested-reading)\nciteturn6search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month;   * 2309.00757: \"A Note on Hamiltonian-Intersecting Families of Graphs\" by Imre Leader, Žarko Ranđelović, Ta Sheng Tan\n\n  * 2208.00117: \"Two-Point Concentration of the Independence Number of the Random Graph\" by Tom Bohman, Jakob Hofstad ... Previous co-organizers:Fall 2019-Fall 2020 Yifan Jing Fall 2019-Fall 2021 Felix Clemen Spring 2022-Spring 2024 Bob Krueger\n\ncombinatorics@uiuc\n\n# Suggested Reading\n\nThe following papers (given by arXiv numbers) are ones we would like to see presented in the seminar. Any student unsure of what to present should look through these papers for something that interests them. (This list is in no particular order and is not comprehensive. Suggestions are welcome to Bob at rak5@illinois.edu)\n\n  * 2208.11181: \"Ramsey numbers upon vertex deletion\" by Yuval Wigderson\n\n  * 2208.10572: \"Balanced supersaturation and Turan numbers in random graphs\" by Tao Jiang, Sean Longbrake\n\n  * 2208.14266: \"Sets Avoiding Full-Rank Three-Point Patterns in (F^n_q)^k Are Exponentially Small\" by Mohamed Omar\n\n  * 2208.14568: \"A remark on the Ramsey number of the hypercube\" by Konstantin Tikhomirov\n\n  * 2209.05147: \"A new upper bound on the minimum degree of minimal Ramsey graphs\" by Anurag Bishnoi, Thomas Lesgourgues\n\n  * 2212.04397: \"The optimal edge-colouring threshold\" by Peter Keevash\n\n  * 2309.00757: \"A Note on Hamiltonian-Intersecting Families of Graphs\" by Imre Leader, Žarko Ranđelović, Ta Sheng Tan\n\n  * 2208.00117: \"Two-Point Concentration of the Independence Number of the Random Graph\" by Tom Bohman, Jakob Hofstad\n\n  * 2305.18252: \"On MaxCut and the Lovász theta function\" by Igor Balla, Oliver Janzer, Benny Sudakov\n\n  * 2309.05468: \"Universality for graphs of bounded degeneracy\" by Peter Allen, Julia Böttcher, Anita Liebenau\n\n  * 2309.04460: \"Essentially tight bounds for rainbow cycles in proper edge-colourings\" by Noga Alon, Matija Bucić, Lisa Sauermann, Dmitrii Zakharov, Or Zamir\n\n  * 2308.15387: \"The power of many colours\" by Noga Alon, Matija Bucić, Micha Cristoph, Michael Krivelevich\n\nPrevious co-organizers:Fall 2019-Fall 2020 Yifan Jing Fall 2019-Fall 2021 Felix Clemen Spring 2022-Spring 2024 Bob Krueger--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCongruences for the partition function $\\text{PDO}_t(n)$ modulo powers of $2$ and $3 | Request PDF (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/372248524_Congruences_for_the_partition_function_textPDO_tn_modulo_powers_of_2_and_3)\nciteturn6search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.4 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago;   * July 2023\n\nDOI:10.48550/arXiv.2307.04687 ... We prove several congruences modulo small powers of 2 and discuss the existence of congruences modulo arbitrary powers of 2 similar to those in Lin's conjecture.\nPreprint\n\n# Congruences for the partition function $\\text{PDO}_t(n)$ modulo powers of 2 and $3\n\n  * July 2023\n\nDOI:10.48550/arXiv.2307.04687\n\nAuthors:\n\nGurinder Singh\n\n  * Hebei Normal University\n\nRupam Barman\n\n  * Indian Institute of Technology Guwahati\n\nImage: Request Full-text Paper PDF\n\nRequest file PDF\n\nTo read the file of this research, you can request a copy directly from the authors.\n\nPreprints and early-stage research may not have been peer reviewed yet.\n\n## Abstract\n\nLin introduced the partition function $\\text{PDO}_t(n)$, which counts the total number of tagged parts over all the partitions of n with designated summands in which all parts are odd. For $k\\geq0$, Lin conjectured congruences for $\\text{PDO}_t(8\\cdot3^kn)$ and $\\text{PDO}_t(12\\cdot3^kn)$ modulo $3^{k+2}$. In this article, we develop a new approach to study these congruences. We study the generating functions of $\\text{PDO}_t(8\\cdot3^kn)$ and $\\text{PDO}_t(12\\cdot3^kn)$ modulo $3^{k+3}$ for certain values of k. We also study $\\text{PDO}_t(n)$ modulo powers of 2. We establish infinitely many congruences for $\\text{PDO}_t(n)$ modulo 8 and 32. We prove several congruences modulo small powers of 2 and discuss the existence of congruences modulo arbitrary powers of 2 similar to those in Lin's conjecture. In reference to this, we also pose some problems for future work.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nau:Barman_R in:math - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?q=au%3ABarman_R+in%3Amath)\nciteturn6search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Apr 06 2026 math.NT arXiv:2604.02723v1 ... By employing properties of the discriminant of reciprocal polynomials, we partially prove a conjecture proposed by Jones in $2021$. ... Jul 11 2023 math.NT arXiv:2307.04687v1\n\n  * Congruences for the partition function $\\text{PDO}_t(n)$ modulo powers of $2$ and $3$\n\nGurinder Singh, Rupam Barman\n\nJul 11 2023 math.NT arXiv:2307.04687v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2307.04687, author = {Gurinder Singh and Rupam Barman}, title = {{C}ongruences for the partition function $\\text{{PDO}}_t(n)$ modulo powers of $2$ and $3$}, year = {2023}, eprint = {2307.04687}, note = {arXiv:2307.04687v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nLin introduced the partition function $\\text{PDO}_t(n)$, which counts the total number of tagged parts over all the partitions of $n$ with designated summands in which all parts are odd. For $k\\geq0$, Lin conjectured congruences for $\\text{PDO}_t(8\\cdot3^kn)$ and $\\text{PDO}_t(12\\cdot3^kn)$ modulo $3^{k+2}$. In this article, we develop a new approach to study these congruences. We study the generating functions of $\\text{PDO}_t(8\\cdot3^kn)$ and $\\text{PDO}_t(12\\cdot3^kn)$ modulo $3^{k+3}$ for certain values of $k$. We also study $\\text{PDO}_t(n)$ modulo powers of $2$. We establish infinitely many congruences for $\\text{PDO}_t(n)$ modulo $8$ and $32$. We prove several congruences modulo small powers of $2$ and discuss the existence of congruences modulo arbitrary powers of $2$ similar to those in Lin's conjecture. In reference to this, we also pose some problems for future work.\n\n  * Parity distribution and divisibility of Mex-related partition functions\n\nSubhrajyoti Bhattacharyya, Rupam Barman, Ajit Singh, Apu Kumar Saha\n\nMar 08 2023 math.NT arXiv:2303.03647v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2303.03647, author = {Subhrajyoti Bhattacharyya and Rupam Barman and Ajit Singh and Apu Kumar Saha}, title = {{P}arity distribution and divisibility of {M}ex-related partition functions}, year = {2023}, eprint = {2303.03647}, note = {arXiv:2303.03647v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSearch | arXiv e-print repository (https://slimjectvitamins.com/accel-vitamin-b-complex-capsules/?_=%2Fsearch%2F%23CD3DgejCa2b3OIySVEszRQc%3D&query=Barman%2C+R&searchtype=author)\nciteturn6search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; By employing properties of the discriminant of reciprocal polynomials, we partially prove a conjecture proposed by Jones in $2021$. ... Submitted 29 January, 2026; originally announced January 2026. ...   44. arXiv:2308.08907  [pdf, ps, other] ...   47. arXiv:2307.04687  [pdf, ps, other]\nFor a given form, we investigate whether this happens for all but finitely many $p$. We consider the more general question when forms have coefficients in Dedekind domains, and, under certain conditions, we prove that the analogous statement holds. However, we also give examples of integral forms for which the answer is negative, and it is crucial that the degree of such forms is composite. We conjecture that we cannot find such examples with forms of prime degree having sufficiently many variables, which is indeed the case when the degree is 2, 3, or 5. Our innovation is to consider the problem in terms of varieties defined by forms, thereby using the tools from algebraic geometry, making their first appearance in this setting. We construct integral forms such that the ratio set of its values is dense in at least one $\\Q_p$ but only in finitely many of them. △ Less\n\nSubmitted 14 July, 2026; v1 submitted 17 August, 2023; originally announced August 2023.\n\nReport number: MPIM-Bonn-2025\n\n  45. arXiv:2308.00027  [pdf, other] \nSubmitted 22 July, 2023; originally announced July 2023.\n\nComments: 25 pages\n\n  47. arXiv:2307.04687  [pdf, ps, other] \n\nmath.NT\n\nCongruences for the partition function $\\text{PDO}_t(n)$ modulo powers of $2$ and $3$\n\nAuthors: Gurinder Singh, Rupam Barman\n\nAbstract: Lin introduced the partition function $\\text{PDO}_t(n)$, which counts the total number of tagged parts over all the partitions of $n$ with designated summands in which all parts are odd. For $k\\geq0$, Lin conjectured congruences for $\\text{PDO}_t(8\\cdot3^kn)$ and $\\text{PDO}_t(12\\cdot3^kn)$ modulo $3^{k+2}$. In this article, we develop a new approach to study these congruences. We study the genera… ▽ More Lin introduced the partition function $\\text{PDO}_t(n)$, which counts the total number of tagged parts over all the partitions of $n$ with designated summands in which all parts are odd. For $k\\geq0$, Lin conjectured congruences for $\\text{PDO}_t(8\\cdot3^kn)$ and $\\text{PDO}_t(12\\cdot3^kn)$ modulo $3^{k+2}$. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nArxiver (https://arxiver.lazybrains.com/author/166126)\nciteturn6search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; We also show that a family of graphs such that any two have Hamiltonian intersection has size at most $1/2^n$ of all graphs, verifying a conjecture of the above authors. ... arXiv:2309.00757\n#### A Note on Hamiltonian-Intersecting Families of Graphs\nImre Leader, Žarko Ranđelović, Ta Sheng Tan\n\nHow many graphs on an $n$-point set can we find such that any two have connected intersection? Berger, Berkowitz, Devlin, Doppelt, Durham, Murthy and Vemuri showed that the maximum is exactly $1/2^{n-1}$ of all graphs. Our aim in this short note is to give a 'directed' version of this result; we show that a family of oriented graphs such that any two have strongly-connected intersection has size at most $1/3^n$ of all oriented graphs. We also show that a family of graphs such that any two have Hamiltonian intersection has size at most $1/2^n$ of all graphs, verifying a conjecture of the above authors.\n\n5 pages\n\narXiv:2309.00757\nCreated: 2023-09-01\n\n#### Galois groups of certain even octic polynomials\nMalcolm Hoong Wai Chen, Angelina Yan Mui Chin, Ta Sheng Tan\n\nLet $f(x)=x^8+ax^4+b \\in \\mathbb{Q}[x]$ be an irreducible polynomial where $b$ is a square. We give a method that completely describes the factorization patterns of a linear resolvent of $f(x)$ using simple arithmetic conditions on $a$ and $b$. As a result, we determine the exact six possible Galois groups of $f(x)$ and completely classify all of them. As an application, we characterize the Galois groups of irreducible polynomials $x^8+ax^4+1 \\in \\mathbb{Q}[x]$. We also use similar methods to obtain analogous results for the Galois groups of irreducible polynomials $x^8+ax^6+bx^4+ax^2+1 \\in \\mathbb{Q}[x]$.\n\n22 pages, preprint of article accepted to Journal of Algebra and Its Applications\n\narXiv:2210.10257 - doi: 10.1142/S0219498823502638\nCreated: 2022-10-18\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nArxiver (https://arxiver.lazybrains.com/author/1077440)\nciteturn6search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; We also show that a family of graphs such that any two have Hamiltonian intersection has size at most $1/2^n$ of all graphs, verifying a conjecture of the above authors. ... arXiv:2